WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.342
A threat hunt can produce several valuable outcomes.

00:00:03.342 --> 00:00:05.774
Confirmed suspicious activity is one possibility,

00:00:05.774 --> 00:00:12.259
but it is not the only measure of success and should never be promised in advance.

00:00:12.259 --> 00:00:15.096
A hunt may uncover a telemetry gap.

00:00:15.096 --> 00:00:19.149
Important systems may not be reporting, fields may be missing,

00:00:19.149 --> 00:00:23.201
retention may be too short, or timestamps may be inconsistent.

00:00:23.201 --> 00:00:27.254
Correcting those issues improves both future hunts and routine monitoring.

00:00:27.254 --> 00:00:30.091
The investigation may reveal a detection opportunity.

00:00:30.091 --> 00:00:35.765
A useful pattern can be translated into a documented analytic, tested against historical data,

00:00:35.765 --> 00:00:39.818
reviewed for false positives, and incorporated into the monitoring program.

00:00:39.818 --> 00:00:43.061
Hunters may also identify asset and ownership gaps.

00:00:43.061 --> 00:00:45.898
Unknown systems, unclear business roles, outdated inventories,

00:00:45.898 --> 00:00:50.761
and ambiguous support responsibilities can obstruct analysis and indicate broader security-governance problems.

00:00:50.761 --> 00:00:53.193
Even normal activity can provide value.

00:00:53.193 --> 00:00:56.435
Understanding expected administrative tools, service relationships, identity patterns,

00:00:56.435 --> 00:00:57.246
maintenance schedules,

00:00:57.246 --> 00:01:02.515
and cloud operations helps analysts recognize meaningful deviations more accurately in the future.

00:01:02.515 --> 00:01:05.352
When evidence suggests an active security event,

00:01:05.352 --> 00:01:10.215
the outcome should include a controlled handoff to the appropriate incident process.

00:01:10.215 --> 00:01:12.241
The hunt record provides context,

00:01:12.241 --> 00:01:15.484
but response decisions belong within established organizational procedures.

00:01:15.484 --> 00:01:19.131
Hunt reporting should distinguish fact, interpretation, limitation, and recommendation.

00:01:19.131 --> 00:01:21.563
Leaders need a concise risk view,

00:01:21.563 --> 00:01:26.021
while analysts and engineers need enough detail to improve data, detections,

00:01:26.021 --> 00:01:27.237
and defensive controls.

00:01:27.237 --> 00:01:32.101
Over time, organizations can measure the improvements produced by hunting: gaps resolved,

00:01:32.101 --> 00:01:35.748
detections created or tuned, coverage increased, recurring questions automated,

00:01:35.748 --> 00:01:38.180
and lessons incorporated into future hypotheses.
